Here's the clean list to work through, in order:
Custom Validation Assignments

1. @AllowedEmailDomain — parameterized constraint
Only allow emails from specific domains, configurable per usage:
java@AllowedEmailDomain(domains = {"gmail.com", "outlook.com"})
private String email;
Introduces: constraint attributes + initialize() method.


2. @FieldsMatch — class-level / cross-field validation
Add emailConfirmation to UserRequest; validate it matches email.
Introduces: @Target(TYPE), validating a whole object instead of one field.


3. @UniqueEmail — validator with a Spring-managed dependency
Reject registration if the email already exists in the DB.
Introduces: @Component validators, constructor-injecting UserRepository, Spring resolving validators from the app context instead of instantiating them by hand.


4. Composed constraint — bundling multiple rules into one
Create @ValidPassword combining @NotBlank + @Size(min=8) + a custom "must contain a digit" rule, reported as a single error.
Introduces: constraint composition, @ReportAsSingleViolation.


5. @UniqueElements — validating a collection
Add List<String> nicknames to UserRequest; reject duplicates (case-insensitive).
Introduces: ConstraintValidator<A, List<String>>, validating collection contents.


6. Method-level validation — validating a @RequestParam directly
Add GET /api/users/search?minId=0; minId must be @Positive.
Introduces: @Validated on the controller class, ConstraintViolationException (new exception type needing its own handler in GlobalExceptionHandler).


7. Validation groups — conditional validation
Make email required on create but optional on a future update endpoint.
Introduces: marker interfaces (OnCreate, OnUpdate), groups = attribute, @Validated(OnCreate.class).
